xt":"Mártires de León"}},{"type":"text","text":" played guitar on «Mal de amores» and on «Bachata en Kingston», which fuses Jamaican dub with Dominican bachata guitar, and the artwork, by the Dominican painter Nadal Walcot, drew on Cocolo culture. The «A la Mar» tour ran through more than forty cities between 2016 and 2018, García recorded a Tiny Desk Concert for NPR Music in February 2018, and the Los Angeles Times later named the album one of the ten best Latin records of the decade. At the 2017 Latin Grammy Awards it won Best New Artist, Best Singer-Songwriter Album, and Best Tropical Song for «Bachata en Kingston»."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"«Candela» and after","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"«Candela» (2019), again produced with Cabra — his partner as well in the electronic project Trending Tropics — runs from the bachata-trap of «Ahí ahí» to the Taíno elegy «Maguá» and «Palm Beach», his first song in English. The Candela Tour reached the United States, Europe and Latin America through 2019. His music video for «Dulcito e’ coco» won Video of the Year at the 2018 Premios Soberano, «Camino al sol», from the 2022 album «Camino al Sol», won Song of the Year at the Soberano and then Best Folk Album at the 2023 Latin Grammy Awards, and «Puñito de Yocahú» (2025) won Best Contemporary Tropical Album at the 2025 Latin Grammy Awards. «Desde Otro Malecón» came out in 2023."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Legacy","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"García built a career out of treating bachata and Dominican folk forms — priprí, palo, gagá, the Congos de Villa Mella — as material for a singer-songwriter’s work rather than a fixed style, and carried that fusion from Brooklyn recording studios to villages in Villa Mella and back onto international stages, on the strength of five Latin Grammy Awards to date."}]}]}'::jsonb, 